Find the Safe Employees — Who Can Leave the Cycle?

A large IT company in Chennai has n employees numbered 0 to n-1. Each employee has a list of colleagues they report to or pass work to. This forms a directed network (graph).

The HR team needs to identify "safe employees" — employees who are not stuck in any work loop (cycle) and whose entire work chain eventually ends at an employee who has no further dependencies (a terminal node).

In simple terms:



If you start from an employee and follow their work chain, do you eventually reach a dead end (no more dependencies)?
If yes → that employee is SAFE
If the chain goes in circles forever → that employee is UNSAFE


Your task: Find all safe employees and print them in ascending order.

If no employee is safe, print No safe employees found.

Input format :
The first line contains two integers n and e — number of employees and number of directed connections
The next e lines each contain two integers u v — meaning employee u passes work to employee v
Output format :
Print all safe employee numbers in ascending order, space-separated on one line

If no employee is safe, print No safe employees found

Code constraints :
1 <= n <= 104
0 <= e <= 104
0 <= u, v < n
The graph may contain self-loops and cycles
Sample test cases :
Input 1 :
7 8
0 1
0 2
1 2
1 3
2 5
3 0
4 5
6 6
Output 1 :
2 4 5
Input 2 :
4 2
0 1
2 3
Output 2 :
0 1 2 3